-- title:
--   Nonzero primes of a domain integral over F[x] are maximal
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $D$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and carries an $R$-algebra structure. Assume that for every $r \in R$ the image $\mathrm{algebraMap}_{R,D}(r)$ is either $0$ or a unit of $D$. Let $x \in D$ be such that every element $d \in D$ is integral over the $R$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\,R\,\{x\} \subseteq D$ generated by $x$, i.e. satisfies a monic polynomial equation with coefficients in that subalgebra. Finally let $P$ be a prime ideal of $D$ with $P \neq \bot$, that is, $P$ is not the zero ideal. The conclusion is that $P$ is a maximal ideal of $D$. In other words, under the stated hypotheses the domain $D$ has Krull dimension at most one: no nonzero prime of $D$ is properly contained in another proper ideal.
--
--   This is the standard dimension-one statement for a domain that is integral over a one-generator algebra over a field: the hypothesis on $R$ forces the image of $R$ in $D$ to be a field, whose fraction field $F$ makes $D$ integral over $F[x]$, a ring of dimension one. It is used in the analysis of the two-chart integral models of modular curves, where it shows that a nonzero prime of a special-fibre chart algebra is already maximal; it is cited in the identification of the ideals pulled back along the chart maps at full level.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_IsMaximal_of_isPrime_of_ne_bot_of_isIntegral_adjoin_singleton.lean

